> > > > > > > > I’m also interested in a comparison between abundance values from two different years’ data.  I followed the methods in section 3.6.5 from Introduction to Distance Sampling (Buckland et al. 2011).  Specifically, I followed the formula for a shared source of variance (equations 3.108 to 3.111).  I noted that this slightly increased the precision to detect differences when compared to the method that assumes complete independence between the two estimates (eqns 3.99 to 3.101).  Intuitively this did not make sense since I was used to variance calculations, that included common sources of variance, to slightly increase the CV when adding separate abundance estimates.  Conceptually, it makes sense that a common source of variance would increase the precision for determining the difference between two estimates but it would be nice to have some sort of affirmation that I haven’t messed up the calculations.
